  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how beneficial soil microbes support crop production and environmental remediation around plant roots. It presents the rhizosphere as a hub where different bacteria and fungi perform distinct but connected functions.\n\nOne major theme is climate regulation: methanotrophs and methanogens are shown influencing methane, while other microbes affect nitrous oxide, indicating roles in greenhouse-gas cycling. A second theme is nutrient cycling, where organisms such as Rhizobium, Pseudomonas, and Nitrosomonas appear to drive nitrogen transformations among nitrate, ammonium, and atmospheric nitrogen, helping make nutrients available to plants.\n\nThe central root-focused section highlights plant growth promotion and stress tolerance. Microbes associated with roots are linked to water uptake, aquaporin activity, phosphate mobilization, and production of plant hormones such as gibberellic acid and zeatin, suggesting improved growth and resilience.\n\nAnother prominent function is pest and disease control. The figure shows microbes used against harmful organisms including beetles, moths, and cyst nematodes, illustrating biological control in the root zone. Finally, the right side depicts degradation of pollutants, where fungi and bacteria break down compounds such as phenol, chlorpyrifos, and penicillin, emphasizing soil cleanup and detoxification. Overall, the image portrays the rhizosphere microbiome as essential for sustainable agriculture.",
